Definitions:
1) What is Parquet:
  Apache Parquet is an open-source, column-oriented data storage format designed for high-performance analytics, 
  optimized for both storage efficiency and rapid read throughput in distributed and local computing environments.
  
  Unlike row-oriented formats (such as CSV, JSON, or relational database row stores) which serialize data sequentially row by    row,Parquet organizes and stores data column by column.
  
  Core Technical Architecture
  Columnar Storage & Layout:
  
  Data is partitioned into horizontal segments called Row Groups, within which data is stored continuously by column chunks.
  
  This structure ensures that values of the same data type are stored contiguously on disk, maximizing locality of reference.
  
  Column-Level Encoding & Compression:
  
  Because data of the same type lives together in a column chunk, Parquet applies highly efficient, type-specific encoding     algorithms (such as dictionary encoding, run-length encoding, and bit-packing) before applying compression codecs (such as Snappy, Gzip, or Zstandard). This drastically reduces disk footprint compared to text-based formats.
